Primary Paper Citations —
Recurrent Latent-Space
Reasoning

Pulled directly from the BDH-CQ paper's own
reference list (arXiv:2608.09888), verified against
arXiv, not from secondhand summaries. All fall within
2022–2026 as required.

1. Hao, S., Sukhbaatar, S., Su, D., et al.(2024)
"Training Large Language Models to Reason in a
Continuous Latent Space" (introduces "Coconut")
arXiv:2412.06769 — https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.06769

Where to cite it: beside any claim that a Transformer-
based LLM can be trained to replace verbal chain-of-
thought steps with continuous hidden-state feedback.

This is the paper BDH-CQ itself cites as the seminal
demonstration of continuous hidden-state feedback in
an autoregressive model, and BDH-CQ explicitly
positions itself as exploring a different regime from
Coconut's staged-curriculum approach.

2. Geiping, J., McLeish, S., Jain, N., et al.
(2025)
"Scaling up Test-Time Compute with Latent
Reasoning: A Recurrent Depth Approach"
arXiv:2502.05171 — https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.05171

Where to cite it: beside any claim about recurrent-
depth models repeatedly applying a shared block to a

latent state to improve reasoning with additional
iterations — i.e., the "turn the effort dial" mechanic
your demo visualizes. BDH-CQ cites this as an
example of recurrent-depth architectures used
primarily for parameter-efficient test-time compute

scaling, distinct from BDH-CQ's demonstration-
dependent learning focus.

3. Saunshi, N., Dikkala, N., Li, Z., Kumar, S.,
Reddi, S.J.(2025)
"Reasoning with Latent Thoughts: On the Power of
Looped Transformers"
arXiv:2502.17416 — https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.17416
Where to cite it: beside any theoretical claim about
why iterative latent computation can substitute for
depth/parameters — this paper provides theoretical
grounding for looped/recurrent architectures
simulating bounded chain-of-thought computation
without per-step parameters.

